 ## Overview
-A method for volumetric rendering that represents a scene as a set of 3D Gaussian distributions, parameterized by mean (position), covariance (shape/direction), and color, allowing for efficient simulation of 3D structure details without meshing [S2, unofficial]. It differs from traditional mesh-based reconstruction by avoiding meshing processes and from NeRF by offering faster training and rendering speeds while maintaining comparable quality [S2, unofficial]. Unlike other kernel methods like Epanechnikov, Gaussian kernels have infinite support, which requires clipping to maintain efficiency [S1, unofficial].
+Gaussian Kernel Volumetric Primitive represents a 3D scene as a collection of volumetric primitives, where each primitive is a 3D Gaussian distribution parameterized by mean, covariance, and color [S2, unofficial]. Unlike standard splatting methods that project Gaussians to 2D ellipses for rasterization, this method treats the Gaussian as a continuous density field in 3D space, enabling physically-based volume rendering (e.g., scattering, emission) via ray tracing [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently simulating 3D structure details and volumetric effects without requiring explicit meshing or expensive neural network evaluations [S2, unofficial]. It is distinguished from its parent [Gaussian Interpolation](2347-gaussian-interpolation.md) by its explicit use of the 3D Gaussian PDF as a volumetric density function rather than just an interpolation kernel, and from [Gaussian Reconstruction Filter](2852-gaussian-reconstruction-filter.md) by its focus on forward volumetric rendering and ray-marching integration [S1, unofficial][S3].
 
