+### Gaussian Voxel Kernel Functions (GVKF) Rendering
+GVKF modifies standard 3DGS alpha blending by replacing the 2D Gaussian projection $G_i^{2D}$ with a 1D kernel function $K_i(0)$ evaluated at the ray origin, allowing the recovery of a continuous opacity density field along the ray [S3]. It uses a constant value $\beta_i$ for opacity and a width parameter $k_i$ for the 1D kernel [S3]. It solves the pitfall of standard 3DGS, which discards the third row of the covariance matrix, collapsing the influence range to an infinitesimal value and preventing the recovery of a continuous opacity density field [S3]. However, the peak of the opacity density derivative $\Phi'(u)$ appears before the actual surface ($u_0 < 0$), requiring the solution of a transcendental equation to locate the surface [S3].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Rendering is efficient, achieving real-time performance [S2, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the number of Gaussians $N$ [S2, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Achieves high-quality real-time view synthesis at 1080p resolution with a frame rate of ≥ 100 fps (or ≥ 30 fps in other cited contexts) for unbounded, complete scenes [S2, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Faster training and rendering speeds compared to NeRF [S2, unofficial]. Does not require meshing [S2, unofficial]. Integrates with physical-based rendering engines (scattering, emissive media) [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Infinite support of Gaussian kernels requires clipping, reducing efficiency compared to finite-support kernels like Epanechnikov [S1, unofficial]. Epanechnikov kernels may be faster and better for sharp edges [S1, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** Rendering time depends on the number of active Gaussians per ray. With spatial partitioning (e.g., BVH), it can be sub-linear in the number of primitives. The closed-form integration using erf is $O(1)$ per primitive [S1, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of Gaussian primitives. Each primitive stores mean, covariance, color, and opacity [S2, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** Achieves high-quality real-time view synthesis at 1080p resolution with a frame rate of $\ge 100$ fps (or $\ge 30$ fps in some contexts) for unbounded, complete scenes [S2, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Faster training and rendering speeds compared to NeRF, while maintaining good or similar quality [S2, unofficial]. Does not require meshing, allowing efficient rendering of point cloud data [S2, unofficial]. Physically-based rendering capabilities for scattering and emission [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Infinite support of Gaussian kernels requires clipping, which can introduce artifacts if not handled carefully [S1, unofficial]. Standard 3DGS rendering discards depth information in the covariance, making it difficult to recover continuous opacity density fields without modifications like GVKF [S3].
 
 ## Background
-3D Gaussian Splatting is a method for 3D reconstruction and rendering that represents a point cloud as a set of 3D Gaussian distributions [S2, unofficial]. It efficiently simulates 3D structure details without meshing [S2, unofficial]. The approach is used in 3D reconstruction and rendering [S2, unofficial]. It is part of the Gaussian Splatting family, which aims to provide faster alternatives to Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) [S2, unofficial].
+3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced as a method for 3D reconstruction and rendering that represents a point cloud as a set of 3D Gaussian distributions [S2, unofficial]. It was recognized with the SIGGRAPH 2023 Best Paper award [S2, unofficial]. The method was developed to address the limitations of traditional mesh-based reconstruction and neural radiance fields (NeRF), offering faster training and rendering speeds [S2, unofficial]. The Gaussian Kernel Volumetric Primitive variant extends this by enabling physically-based volume rendering [S1, unofficial].
 
